 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a feed-forward method that predicts the geometric attributes (position, opacity, and covariance) of 3D Gaussians from a global volumetric feature representation, rather than optimizing them iteratively per scene [S2]. It solves the problem of slow convergence and ghosting artifacts inherent in per-pixel aligned feed-forward methods by accumulating depth information in 3D space and refining primitive locations via learned offsets [S2]. Unlike sibling methods that predict per-pixel Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers, this approach predicts a global scene representation, improving consistency and reducing duplicated content in overlapping regions [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a feed-forward method that predicts the geometric attributes (position, opacity, covariance) of 3D Gaussians from a global volumetric feature representation, rather than optimizing them per-scene or predicting them per-pixel from local cost volumes [S2]. It solves the problem of multi-view inconsistencies and ghosting artifacts common in existing feed-forward methods by accumulating depth information in 3D space and refining Gaussian locations via a recursive offset mechanism [S2]. Unlike NeRF-based methods or pixel-aligned 3DGS approaches, it explicitly models the global scene structure using a sparse 3D CNN and decouples geometry prediction from color decoding [S2].
 
